{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787127402,"nonce":"IOd/QkmSWY67u4ZYssTcqgFGs9w=","message":"⚠️ **CORRECTION TO MY OWN CLOSE, SAME SESSION. I closed this ticket before it was done.**\n\nThe comment above claimed every clause of the Done-when was met. It was not. I removed the STATE and left every promise of the TRANSITION INTO it standing — twenty-seven places still asserting, as fact, that closing a generation freezes its Slack thread. The commit is amended (now `4784fd0`) and this is what was missing.\n\n**Two of the twenty-seven were not source comments at all, which is why this matters more than tidiness.**\n\n- **`db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:89` is a LIVE COLUMN COMMENT.** It reads: *\"Idle past orgs.settings.group_close_delay_s closes the group and freezes its thread.\"* A column comment lives IN THE DATABASE. `\\d+ alert_groups` prints it to an operator today, and editing the source file changes nothing that is already deployed — only a `COMMENT ON` statement does. 00066 now carries that correction, and its Down restores the old sentence. This is the same shape as the defect this ticket was filed about: `00011:197` promising a state nothing could enter. I fixed that one and missed its twin one table over.\n- **`api/openapi/openapi.yaml:10020` is operator-facing CONTRACT text.** It justifies the rule \"keep `group_close_delay_s` at or above `refire_grace`\" with the freeze. So did `docs/setup/tuning.md:411`, `docs/adr/0026:135`, and `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:168` — the settings-screen warning.\n\n⭐ **The guidance was right; the mechanism was wrong. So the guidance is kept everywhere and the mechanism is corrected.** A closed generation is not sealed — nothing seals it, and nothing ever did. What actually keeps a re-fire off the old card is that `channel_threads.subject_id` **is** the generation row, so the next observation opens generation N+1 and a new generation is a NEW thread. `00011`'s own comment on that column already said exactly this, which is how the replacement wording was verified rather than invented. `grouping/service/service.go:466` had both readings in one comment — the false mechanism and the true one — in consecutive sentences; the true one is now the only one.\n\n**Also corrected in the same pass:**\n\n- **`docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:172`** published the *implemented* gate order as \"dead → frozen → sequence → root\" under **AMENDED**. That is the one document an implementer reads for what was actually built, so it would have taught a gate arm that no longer compiles.\n- **`docs/runbooks/oto_thread_gap_recovered_total.md:46`** told on-call, mid-incident, to check whether the thread is \"marked dead or frozen\".\n- **ADR 0023 is now marked `⚠️ NARROWED`, not `⛔ SUPERSEDED IN HALF`.** Every `⛔` precedent in this repo marks a claim proven FALSE; nothing in 0023 is falsified — one of its two terminal states simply left. The **Status:** line carries it too, because 0020, 0012, 0030 and 0036 all record supersession there, and an annotation at line 52 of 129 is invisible to anyone reading the header or the ADR index.\n- **00066 now spends the expand/contract exemption properly.** Narrowing a CHECK shipped in 00011 contradicts §D. `00059` and `00060` each wrote a `⛔⛔` paragraph for that and each said, in terms, that its header **is not a precedent for the next narrowing** — so 00066 re-verifies the facts instead of citing them: `git tag` lists nothing, so there is no release N to be incompatible with, and the moment a tagged release exists the exemption is spent. It also notes this is the mildest of the three, because 00059 and 00060 removed values that had been written and no row ever held `frozen`.\n- `thread.go`'s new comment defended keeping `Terminal()` as a predicate by citing \"the two call sites\". There is exactly **one**. That was a fresh instance of the defect class this ticket exists to remove, in the commit removing it. Fixed, and it now records the asymmetry rather than hiding it: the `platform/jobs/ordering` mirror tests its state inline instead, and harmonising that would be a second change riding along inside a deletion.\n\n⚠️ **One finding is accepted and deliberately NOT fixed, so it is recorded rather than buried.** `internal/platform/jobs/ordering/ordering.go`'s state switch has no `default`. Apply 00066's **Down** — which the new integration assertion explicitly proves re-admits `frozen` — have the rolled-back release write `state='frozen'`, and a still-running new binary returns `proceed` where it used to return `abandon`/`thread_frozen`, posting into a thread the other half of the fleet considers closed. The fix is not a line: `Decide`'s verdict vocabulary is declared CLOSED and every verdict is a label on `oto_thread_order_decisions_total{action,reason}`, so a new `unknown_state` reason drags in the runbook table and the SPEC §G.7 verdict table. That is chunk-sized. The harm also needs a mixed-version fleet, which the no-installs position rules out. **Note it was ALREADY fall-through for a genuinely unknown state — this change narrowed the vocabulary, it did not open the hole.** Worth its own ticket.\n\n**Gate, re-run over the completed change:** all eleven `ci` recipes individually. 9/11 green; the two reds are exactly the two pre-existing baselines (`lint-vocabulary` at 60, unchanged, and `TestNoStateHueInChrome` from `59c7def`) — one `--- FAIL` line in the whole suite. `test/integration` passed, so `TestEveryMigrationDownTo00028IsReversible` exercised **both** column-comment corrections in **both** directions against a real database.\n\n**How this was caught:** a two-lens review of the committed change. Both lenses found it independently and both were right. Recording that because the lesson is not \"a comment was missed\" — it is that removing a state is only half the job, and the half I skipped was the half an operator actually reads.","files":null}]